[2026-07-19T12:38:00.128Z] [INFO]         "content": "Web search results for query: \"npm registry \"eventually consistent\" CDN cache package metadata delay after publish official\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Demystifying npm package installation: Insights, analysis and optimization tips - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/pavel-zeman/demystifying-npm-package-installation-insights-analysis-and-optimization-tips-4nmj\"},{\"title\":\"Clear Cache NPM and Fix Your Workflow for Good\",\"url\":\"https://hostmora.com/blog/clear-cache-npm/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - mixu/npm_lazy: A lazy local cache for NPM to make your local deploys faster · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/mixu/npm_lazy\"},{\"title\":\"[BUG] npx is too slow for already cached package with specific version requested · Issue #7295 · npm/cli\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/npm/cli/issues/7295\"},{\"title\":\"NPM CI massively slower this week · community · Discussion #162536\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/orgs/community/discussions/162536\"},{\"title\":\"[BUG] `npm start` updates cache from custom registry · Issue #6799 · npm/cli\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/npm/cli/issues/6799\"},{\"title\":\"NPM Caching: Speeding Up Your Development Process | by Rubén Alapont | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@ruben.alapont/npm-caching-speeding-up-your-development-process-340dcdc554b3\"},{\"title\":\"`npm cache clear <package>` should remove cached `.npm/<registry>` metadata · Issue #10349 · npm/npm\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/npm/npm/issues/10349\"},{\"title\":\"npm Blog Archive: New npm Registry Architecture\",\"url\":\"https://blog.npmjs.org/post/75707294465/new-npm-registry-architecture.html\"},{\"title\":\"Remove metadata cache and retry install on broken package integrity · Issue #6087 · pnpm/pnpm\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pnpm/pnpm/issues/6087\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding npm registry CDN cache and package metadata delay after publish:\n\nPackage metadata is typically cached with a max-age of about 300 seconds (that's 5 minutes), while the actual package tarballs are often cached for around 31,557,600 seconds—a full year. \n\nThat five-minute metadata delay is exactly why you sometimes can't see a freshly published package right away. This is related to how the npm registry handles caching through its CDN infrastructure.\n\nAccording to the search results, Fastly is the CDN that fronts the registry, and that's what the npm client actually talks to. The caching strategy differs significantly between metadata and package content: Tarballs have a high TTL, since they change rarely, and benefit greatly from caching, whereas metadata requires more frequent updates since new versions are published regularly.\n\nThe eventual consistency model stems from this CDN caching approach where recently published package metadata takes up to approximately 5 minutes to fully propagate through the cached CDN layer to all clients.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."
