[2026-07-19T12:40:22.432Z] [INFO]         "type": "text",
[2026-07-19T12:40:22.432Z] [INFO]         "text": "Research complete. Report below.\n\n---\n\n# Off-the-shelf solutions research\n\n**Method note:** Claims marked **[VERIFIED]** were confirmed by fetching the primary source (docs/repo/issue) directly. Claims marked **[SEARCH-ONLY]** come from search-result summaries I did not independently fetch. Claims marked **[INFERRED]** are my judgment, not sourced. One important correction: the search engine's summary for topic 5 asserted zizmor had shipped a timeout-minutes audit — **this is false**, and fetching the docs disproved it. Treat search summaries with suspicion.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Helm chart publishing from CI\n\n### `helm/chart-releaser-action`\n- **URL:** https://github.com/helm/chart-releaser-action (wraps https://github.com/helm/chart-releaser)\n- **Solves:** Exactly your hand-rolled script. Packages charts, creates GitHub Releases with the `.tgz` as an asset, and generates + commits `index.yaml` to `gh-pages`. **[VERIFIED]** README: *\"A GitHub branch called `gh-pages` to store the published charts\"* is required, and *\"You do not need an `index.yaml` file in `main` at all because it is managed in the `gh-pages` branch.\"*\n- **Maturity:** ~664 stars, latest release **v1.7.0 (Jan 2025)**, official `helm/` org. **[VERIFIED]**\n- **OCI:** Not native. **[VERIFIED]** It has a `skip_upload` option, documented as enabling *\"more advanced uploading of your charts (for exemple with OCI based repositories) which doen't require the `index.yaml`\"* — i.e. OCI is left to you.\n\n### OCI / GHCR via `helm push`\n- **URL:** https://helm.sh/docs/topics/registries/\n- **[VERIFIED]** Helm docs state: *\"It is recommended to use container registries with OCI support to store and share chart packages.\"* That is Helm's own recommendation, in the current docs.\n- **[SEARCH-ONLY]** OCI support went **GA in Helm 3.8.0** (Feb 2022), graduating out of experimental and enabled by default. Consistent across the release notes and the Helm blog, but I did not fetch v3.8.0 release notes directly.\n- **[VERIFIED]** Usage shape: `helm package` → `helm push mychart-0.1.0.tgz oci://ghcr.io/<org>/charts`. The `oci://` ref omits basename and tag; chart name becomes repo basename, semver becomes the tag.\n- Third-party wrappers exist (`appany/helm-oci-chart-releaser`, `JimCronqvist/action-helm-chart-repo`) but are individual-maintainer projects — **[INFERRED]** not worth the dependency when `helm push` is two lines.\n\n### Verdict: which is best practice in 2026?\n**[INFERRED, but grounded]** **OCI/GHCR is the better default**, for reasons I can actually defend:\n1. Helm's own docs recommend OCI registries (verified quote above).\n2. OCI removes the entire failure mode you hit — there is no `index.yaml`, no `gh-pages` branch, no `git push` race, so there is nothing to silently no-op. A failed `helm push` returns non-zero.\n3. It works on private repos without GitHub Enterprise, since GHCR handles auth. **[SEARCH-ONLY]**\n\n**Caveat you should weigh:** the classic `index.yaml` repo is still what ArtifactHub and `helm repo add` consume most conventionally, and OCI charts need `helm registry login` for private registries. Many projects publish **both** — `chart-releaser-action` with `skip_upload: false` for discoverability, plus `helm push` to GHCR. **[INFERRED]**\n\n**Recommendation for your repo:** replace the hand-rolled script with `helm/chart-releaser-action` if you want to keep the gh-pages contract, or drop to plain `helm package && helm push oci://ghcr.io/...` if you're willing to move consumers. Either eliminates the silent-no-op class of bug. Do **not** keep a bespoke `git checkout gh-pages` script.\n\n### `peaceiris/actions-gh-pages` / `stefanzweifel/git-auto-commit-action`\nBoth are generic commit/publish actions and would work as the transport layer, but neither knows anything about Helm — you'd still hand-roll `helm repo index`. **[INFERRED]** Strictly worse than `chart-releaser-action` for this job.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Shell execution with fail-fast semantics\n\nThis is the root cause, and the answer is unambiguous: **`command-stream` is the outlier.** All three mainstream alternatives throw by default.\n\n| Library | Throws on non-zero by default? | Escape hatch | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **`google/zx`** | **Yes** | `.nothrow()` / `$({nothrow: true})` | **[VERIFIED]** |\n| **`execa`** | **Yes** | `reject: false` | **[VERIFIED]** |\n| **`dax-sh`** | **Yes** | `.noThrow()` | **[SEARCH-ONLY]** |\n| **`node:child_process`** | **Depends on API** | n/a | **[INFERRED]** |\n\n**zx** — https://github.com/google/zx, docs https://google.github.io/zx/process-promise\n**[VERIFIED]** The docs describe `nothrow()` as: *\"Changes behavior of `$` to not throw an exception on non-zero exit codes.\"* Throwing is therefore the default; opting out is explicit.\n\n**execa** — https://github.com/sindresorhus/execa, https://github.com/sindresorhus/execa/blob/main/docs/errors.md\n**[VERIFIED]** *\"When the subprocess fails, the promise returned by `execa()` is rejected with an `ExecaError` instance.\"* And: *\"When the `reject` option is `false`, the `error` is returned instead.\"*\n\n**dax** — https://github.com/dsherret/dax\n**[SEARCH-ONLY]** README documents `await $\\`exit 123\\`` producing `Uncaught Error: Exited with code: 123`, with `.noThrow()` to opt out and a `CommandBuilder` to make no-throw the default. I could not fetch the README section directly (WebFetch returned a truncated excerpt), so treat the exact quote as unconfirmed even though the behavior is corroborated by multiple sources.\n\n**`node:child_process`** — https://nodejs.org/api/child_process.html\n**[INFERRED — I did not fetch the Node docs, verify before citing]** Behavior varies by function, which is precisely why it's error-prone:\n- `exec` / `execFile` (callback): pass an `Error` with a `code` property on non-zero — you must check it; nothing throws.\n- `execSync` / `execFileSync`: **throw** on non-zero exit.\n- `spawn`: never throws for non-zero; emits `'close'` with the exit code. Silent failure by default.\n- `spawnSync`: does **not** throw; returns a result object with `status`.\n\n**Recommendation:** **`execa`** is the strongest fit for a Node/npm library repo. **[INFERRED]** It is the most widely depended-upon of the three, has no Deno-first baggage (dax), and doesn't pull in a global-injecting CLI runtime (zx installs globals like `$`, `cd`, `fs` by design). If you prefer the ergonomic template-literal script style for CI scripts specifically, zx is fine and equally fail-fast.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Verifying an npm publish landed\n\n### Is the propagation lag real and documented?\n**Partially.** I found no crisp, authoritative npm-docs sentence stating an SLA on read-after-write. What I did find:\n\n**[SEARCH-ONLY]** Multiple corroborating `npm/cli` issues describing exactly your symptom:\n- **npm/cli#3424** — *\"[BUG] npm is not finding newly published versions of packages\"*; installing the just-published version fails with \"No matching version found.\"\n- **npm/cli#9043** — *\"[BUG] After `npm publish`, `npm pack` cannot find the package\"*; reporter added a retry loop (5 attempts, 1/min) and the tarball was **still** missing after 5 minutes.\n- **npm/cli#593** — chokidar 3.3.1 published, but the npm web page, the JSON API, and the CLI all still reported 3.3.0.\n- **github/community#46463** — *\"When will my published repo be available in the npm registry?\"*\n\n**[SEARCH-ONLY, and I'd flag this as weakly sourced]** The mechanism commonly given is CDN caching: the registry is fronted by Fastly; **package metadata is cached with `max-age` around 300 seconds (5 min)** while tarballs get a ~1-year TTL. That 5-minute metadata TTL is the usual explanation for freshly published versions being invisible. This came from a DEV Community post, not npm — **do not cite it as authoritative.** The *phenomenon* is well-attested by the npm/cli issues; the *5-minute CDN number* is community folklore that happens to match observed behavior.\n\n**Important caveat — don't misdiagnose:** **[SEARCH-ONLY]** a large fraction of \"E404 right after publish\" reports in 2025–2026 are **not** propagation lag. With npm Trusted Publishing, a failed OIDC handshake causes the registry to treat the client as anonymous, and anonymous users can't PUT — which surfaces as a **misleading 404**. This requires **npm CLI ≥ 11.5.1**; Node 22 ships npm v10 and will fail this way, Node 24 ships npm v11. If your CI uses Node 22 with trusted publishing, that is a far more likely root cause than replication lag, and a retry loop will never fix it.\n\n### Existing retry/verify utilities\n**[VERIFIED absence]** I found **no** well-known, purpose-built \"verify npm publish landed\" action or library. The observed community pattern is a hand-rolled poll of `npm view <pkg>@<version> version` with backoff — which is what npm/cli#9043's reporter did, unsuccessfully at 5 minutes.\n\n**Recommendation:** **[INFERRED]**\n1. First confirm you're not hitting the OIDC/404 red herring — pin Node 24 / npm ≥ 11.5.1 in the publish job.\n2. Treat `npm publish` exiting 0 as authoritative for \"did it publish.\" Do not gate the pipeline on a subsequent `npm view` succeeding.\n3. If you must verify, poll with backoff over a **generous** window (≥10 min, not 1 min) and use `npm view --registry=https://registry.npmjs.org/` explicitly, and make a timeout a **warning, not a failure** — otherwise you'll fail builds for publishes that actually succeeded.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Pushing to a busy main branch with rebase-retry\n\n**`stefanzweifel/git-auto-commit-action`** — https://github.com/stefanzweifel/git-auto-commit-action\n**[VERIFIED]** Issue **#170, \"Retry for non-fast-forward pushes\"**, is **CLOSED**. The request was *\"The action should do a pull if it encounters 'non-fast-forward' as push result and then retry.\"* I could **not** retrieve the maintainer's resolution comment, so **I cannot confirm whether it was closed as implemented or as wontfix.** Related issue **#209** (\"Handle failed push error when head falls behind\") also exists. **You should check #170's closing comment before relying on this.**\n\n**`ad-m/github-push-action`** — https://github.com/ad-m/github-push-action\n**[NOT VERIFIED]** I did not fetch this repo. I have no evidence it handles rebase-retry, and I won't guess. Note **[INFERRED]** it is widely regarded as lightly maintained; verify before adopting.\n\n**`cbrgm/git-rebase-push-action`-style actions** — marketplace listing \"Git Rebase Push\"\n**[SEARCH-ONLY]** Described as handling *\"concurrent git commits with automatic rebase retry loop, perfect for GitOps workflows where multiple processes update the same repository simultaneously.\"* This is the only thing I found that targets your exact problem, but I did not verify its maintainer, star count, or release cadence — **[INFERRED]** likely a small single-maintainer action, so audit it before trusting it with pushes to main.\n\n**Verdict:** **[INFERRED]** There is **no mature, well-known action** that robustly does rebase-retry pushes to a protected main. The realistic options are (a) a small hand-rolled retry loop around `git pull --rebase && git push` — which is genuinely the standard fix and is only a few lines, or (b) sidestep the problem entirely with a GitHub Actions **`concurrency:`** group on the publishing workflow so two runs never race for main. **[INFERRED]** Option (b) is architecturally cleaner and I'd recommend it first; a rebase-retry loop is a workaround for contention you could just serialize away.\n\nAlso note **[INFERRED]** `--force-with-lease` + `--force-if-includes` is the safe-force idiom if you ever need it, but it is the wrong tool for appending to a shared main.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Enforcing `timeout-minutes` on every job\n\n**This is where the search engine was wrong, and the answer is \"neither tool does this today.\"**\n\n### zizmor — https://docs.zizmor.sh/audits/\n**[VERIFIED by fetching the full audit list]** There is **NO** `timeout-minutes` audit. The complete audit set is: adhoc-packages, anonymous-definition, archived-uses, artipacked, bot-conditions, cache-poisoning, concurrency-limits, dangerous-triggers, dependabot-cooldown, dependabot-execution, excessive-permissions, forbidden-uses, github-app, github-env, hardcoded-container-credentials, impostor-commit, insecure-commands, known-vulnerable-actions, misfeature, obfuscation, overprovisioned-secrets, ref-confusion, ref-version-mismatch, secrets-inherit, secrets-outside-env, self-hosted-runner, stale-action-refs, superfluous-actions, template-injection, typosquat-uses, undocumented-permissions, unpinned-images, unpinned-tools, unpinned-uses, unredacted-secrets, unsound-condition, unsound-contains, unsound-ternary, use-trusted-publishing.\n\n**[VERIFIED]** zizmor issue **#1023** (\"Feature: Require or recommend the `timeout-minutes` property for all jobs\") is **OPEN**, with an associated **open** PR. So it is requested and in progress, but **not shipped**.\n\n**[VERIFIED]** zizmor **does** cover your other two asks:\n- `unpinned-uses` — *\"Detects 'unpinned' `uses:` clauses… actions should be pinned by SHA reference.\"*\n- `excessive-permissions` — *\"Detects excessive permissions in workflows, both at workflow level and individual job levels.\"* Plus `undocumented-permissions`.\n\n### actionlint — https://github.com/rhysd/actionlint\n**[VERIFIED]** Issue **#49** (\"Feature Request: add rule to force `timeout-minutes`\") is **OPEN**. The issue notes the default is 360 minutes (6h) and omitting it wastes quota. **I could not retrieve any maintainer response**, so I cannot tell you whether rhysd intends to implement it. **No timeout-minutes rule exists in actionlint today.**\n\n**[SEARCH-ONLY]** actionlint also has no per-rule disable mechanism — only an `-ignore` regex option.\n\n### OpenSSF Scorecard\n**[NOT VERIFIED]** I did not fetch Scorecard's check documentation. **[INFERRED]** Scorecard's checks (Token-Permissions, Pinned-Dependencies) map to permissions and pinning, but I have **no evidence** it checks `timeout-minutes`, and I won't assert it. Verify against https://github.com/ossf/scorecard/blob/main/docs/checks.md before citing.\n\n### Recommendation\n**[INFERRED]** Adopt **zizmor** for the security-hardening checks (pinning, permissions) — it's the strongest tool in this space and directly covers two of your three asks. For `timeout-minutes` specifically, you'll need a **custom check today**: a `yq`/JS script over `.github/workflows/*.yml` asserting every job has `timeout-minutes`. Watch zizmor #1023 and delete your custom check when it lands.\n\n**One correctness caveat for whoever writes that check** **[SEARCH-ONLY]**: jobs that call a reusable workflow via `uses:` **do not support `timeout-minutes`** — the property must be set on the jobs inside the called workflow. A naive \"every job must have timeout-minutes\" linter will produce false positives on reusable-workflow callers. Also note GitHub accepts fractional values, though zizmor had a deserialization bug with `timeout-minutes: 0.5` (zizmor issue #1672).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Sentry sourcemaps with no build step\n\n**[VERIFIED]** From https://docs.sentry.io/platforms/javascript/guides/node/sourcemaps/ — the docs are organized entirely around bundlers/transpilers (webpack, Vite, Rollup, esbuild, TypeScript, UglifyJS, SystemJS). The general statement is: *\"To enable readable stack traces in your Sentry errors, you need to upload your source maps to Sentry.\"* For anything outside those tools, the only guidance is: *\"we recommend you upload them using Sentry CLI.\"* There's also: *\"Source maps are only generated and uploaded during production builds. Development builds typically do not generate source maps for upload.\"*\n\n**[VERIFIED absence]** Sentry's docs **do not address** the no-build-step case at all. There is no page saying \"if you don't transpile, skip this.\" The framing throughout presupposes a build artifact that differs from source.\n\n**[INFERRED — this is reasoning, not a citation]** For a plain ESM Node project shipped unbundled and untranspiled, **uploading sourcemaps is not meaningful**. A sourcemap maps *generated* code back to *original* code. If the file Node executes is byte-identical to the file you authored, the mapping is the identity function and carries zero information — stack traces already point at real filenames, real line numbers, and real source. There is nothing to un-minify. Sentry will happily accept an upload; it just won't change any stack trace.\n\n**[SEARCH-ONLY]** A related point worth knowing: the Sentry Node SDK works with the `source-map-support` package as an alternative to uploading maps. That's for the transpiled case, not yours.\n\n**Recommendation:** **[INFERRED]** Drop the sourcemap upload step from the release pipeline. It adds a Sentry auth token, a CI dependency, and a failure mode, in exchange for no debugging benefit. Revisit only if a bundling or transpilation step is ever introduced. If you want Sentry to show source *context* lines, that's a separate feature and is not solved by sourcemaps.\n\n---\n\n## Summary table\n\n| # | Problem | Best off-the-shelf option | Confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Helm publish | `helm push` to `oci://ghcr.io` (Helm's own recommendation), or `helm/chart-releaser-action` to keep gh-pages | High |\n| 2 | Fail-fast shell | **`execa`** (throws by default, verified) — replace `command-stream` | High |\n| 3 | Verify npm publish | **No standard tool exists.** Check the npm ≥11.5.1 OIDC red herring first; treat publish exit-0 as truth | Medium |\n| 4 | Rebase-retry push | **No mature action.** Prefer a `concurrency:` group; hand-rolled retry loop otherwise | Medium |\n| 5 | timeout-minutes lint | **Nothing ships this.** zizmor for pinning/permissions + custom check for timeouts | High (verified negative) |\n| 6 | Sentry sourcemaps | Not applicable — remove the step | Medium-high (inferred) |\n\n## Things I could not verify — do not treat as settled\n- The **closing rationale of git-auto-commit-action #170** (closed, but implemented vs wontfix is unknown).\n- **`ad-m/github-push-action`'s** actual retry behavior — not fetched at all.\n- **OpenSSF Scorecard's** check list — not fetched; my statement about it is inference only.\n- The **5-minute npm CDN `max-age`** figure — community blog, not npm.\n- **`node:child_process`** per-API throw semantics — from my own knowledge, not fetched from nodejs.org.\n- **dax's** exact README wording on `noThrow` — behavior corroborated, quote unconfirmed.\n\n## Sources\n- [helm/chart-releaser-action](https://github.com/helm/chart-releaser-action)\n- [Helm — Use OCI-based registries](https://helm.sh/docs/topics/registries/)\n- [Helm 3.8.0 release](https://github.com/helm/helm/releases/tag/v3.8.0)\n- [zx — ProcessPromise docs](https://google.github.io/zx/process-promise)\n- [execa — errors.md](https://github.com/sindresorhus/execa/blob/main/docs/errors.md)\n- [dsherret/dax](https://github.com/dsherret/dax)\n- [npm/cli#3424 — new versions not found](https://github.com/npm/cli/issues/3424)\n- [npm/cli#9043 — npm pack can't find just-published package](https://github.com/npm/cli/issues/9043)\n- [npm/cli#593 — published version not showing](https://github.com/npm/cli/issues/593)\n- [zizmor audits](https://docs.zizmor.sh/audits/)\n- [zizmor#1023 — timeout-minutes feature request (OPEN)](https://github.com/zizmorcore/zizmor/issues/1023)\n- [actionlint#49 — timeout-minutes rule request (OPEN)](https://github.com/rhysd/actionlint/issues/49)\n- [git-auto-commit-action#170 — non-fast-forward retry (CLOSED)](https://github.com/stefanzweifel/git-auto-commit-action/issues/170)\n- [Sentry — Source Maps for Node.js](https://docs.sentry.io/platforms/javascript/guides/node/sourcemaps/)"
