[2026-07-19T13:15:59.326Z] [INFO]   "summary": "cd /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1784463865325; git add .github/workflows/release.yml scripts/workflow-lint.lib.mjs scripts/docker-pr-build.sh scripts/docker-pr-verify-containers.sh tests/ci-workflow-cancellation-2082.test.mjs .changeset/olive-pans-shout.md && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF' && git push -q origin issue-2082-90c17c1e7d2a && git log --oneline -1\nfix(ci): stop cancelling releases mid-publish, drop always() (#2082)\n\nF14 — the concurrency policy was inverted:\n\n    cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' }}\n\nIt cancelled in-progress runs on main — the one branch that publishes —\nand queued them everywhere else. A release cancelled between `npm\npublish` and the `git push` of the version bump leaves the registry\nahead of the repository: the same split-brain state as F4 and F5,\nreached by a different route. Now main queues and PR branches cancel,\nwhich is also where the wasted runners were.\n\nF13 — nine jobs guarded their `if:` with `always()`, which is true even\nafter a cancellation, so they defeated the policy above. release.yml\ndocuments this rule at the top of the file (from the #1274 and #1278\ncase studies) but applied it only to the Docker jobs. Five used bare\n`always()`; four paired it with a contradictory `!cancelled()`.\n\nVerified semantics with a real YAML parse: exactly 9 of 25 jobs changed,\neach only in the leading term, the other 16 byte-identical. Note that\n`if: !cancelled() && ...` must be wrapped in ${{ }} — a leading `!` is a\nYAML tag, and the previous always() spelling did not need this.\n\nAlso extracts two Docker blocks from release.yml into scripts/. The\ntimeout-minutes added in 0e4eb599 had pushed the file to 1512 lines,\nover the 1500-line limit — which no CI run caught, because the missing\nchangeset failed the changeset gate and every other job was skipped.\nThat changeset is added here too.\nEOF",
